designing a small home is a challenge  for anybody even a fully trained  architect  however today i wanted to share with you  my 10 tips  on how you can design the best small  homes  what's going on guys my name is david  thomich and welcome back to the channel  today we're talking about small homes  small homes are generally defined  as a space that you have no space  it is a very challenging project and it  is always something that i look forward  to when it comes to designing  architecture today i wanted to share  with you my 10 best pieces of advice  for designing a small home or even  renovating your own small home  this way you can absolutely maximize the  space that you have been given the first  thing you need to remember is that space  is luxury you do not have a lot of space  to work with  so make sure that everything is planned  out perfectly  before you go ahead and start working so  if you're renovating a small home for  example you don't want to start on one  section  finish that move on to the next finish  that and move on to the next  you want to map out and plan out the  whole entire space  there isn't much to work with here so  that's exactly why you have to do  the whole thing at once if you're  designing a new small home on the other  hand  it takes a very very long time to plan  out  every single detail sometimes it takes  longer to design a small home that it  takes  to design an entire mansion so when it  comes down to designing small homes  always remember that every detail has to  be planned out  before you begin actually doing any work  the next element to remember is  storage is king storage is absolutely  everything in a small home so wherever  you can store something  make sure it is a storage container for  example  if you have a bed you can have it as a  gas lift bed which is storage underneath  or that same bed can be a murphy bed  that hides away  so you have additional space and  additional storage  any sort of tv cabinet bookcase shelving  unit  coffee tables even you have to select  the right furniture that creates its own  storage elements you don't want to go  out and purchase a nice big lavish  coffee table that looks phenomenal  but at the same time it just takes up a  whole  two square meters in your living room  and does nothing else but is a  placeholder  so you have to get very creative and  very clever  a coffee table shouldn't just be a  coffee table a bookshelf  shouldn't just be a bookshelf a bed  shouldn't just be a bed  you have to really carefully think about  every single piece of furniture  that is going into that house you also  have to think about  every structural element that's in that  house and how you can actually  utilize this space in there a good  example is i  beams and columns if you have a  beautiful column that  shapes right through the center of your  small home it's usually in the shape of  an eye  to simplify things you can utilize that  column and create  bookcases or shelves or any sort of  storage inside it  so it just isn't a random column in the  middle of your floor plan  or tucked away in the corner you have to  get as creative as possible  another scenario is under stair storage  if you have stairs raking up to a loft  you have to make sure you utilize  every step underneath because after all  that space underneath stairs  is wasted space unless you carefully  think about it you can easily create  that understeer storage into some sort  of additional room like a study nook  it really gives you the ability to  multi-purpose that space  which brings us on to our next point  multi-purpose rooms  a bed can be anything but a bed if it's  a murphy bed  it quickly hides away into a wall cavity  and it's disappeared so you can utilize  that space again  the underside of stairs can be converted  into a study nook so it isn't just a  bunch of dead space  walls can be operable they can be  sliding they can be moving they can be  corner fixed  whatever you can do to partition spaces  and create  an illusion of extra space this is what  you want to achieve with multi-purpose  design  so for example if you have two spaces a  living room and a bedroom  with a petition sliding wall in the  middle that murphy bed in the bedroom  could be pushed away and that entire  wall  could be slid across to the edge of that  room so all of a sudden  your living room isn't half the size but  it's the full size  you really have to think about all the  ways rooms can become  multifunctional and multi-purpose now  the next point is purely for inspiration  the japanese do incredible things with  small homes  so if you're looking for inspiration  make sure you research japanese  architecture  and japanese minimalism both of these  can truly redefine your space and  perception of what a small home could be  now when we start thinking about  traditional elements we have to scrap  that entire idea  every single door in your small home  should be sliding  they should slide into cavities and  pockets and they should slide  flush so when you push that cavity  slider  all the way into the wall there isn't  that standard 50 mil  recess that sits out so you can grab the  door there's a little hook  that pops out so you can take it you  want to be able to push that door as  far away as possible because if you  think about a traditional swing door  all of a sudden you have a square meter  in front of that door that's  absolutely dead space you can't do  anything with it  because the door needs to swing that  entire range of motion  whereas with a sliding door you don't  have that problem so  swing doors are a no go in small house  designs just like doors  windows are everything the more windows  you can utilize in a small home the  better  windows give you the optical illusion of  grandeur and space  with a small home it doesn't matter  where you place a window it is going to  be ideal  so think creatively about windows you  don't have to go and place a million and  one windows across the entire front  north facade  and just beacon that light into you you  can go with small highlight windows  or narrow skinny windows in order to  genuinely bring in a lot of  directed light and if you don't have  that ability to bring in natural light  through the external walls think about  the ceiling space and think about the  roof with small designs if you're  starting from scratch you want them to  be as high as possible  if you can utilize voids and space that  again  gives that grand feeling of space even  in a small confined environment  so having a double story void or having  a story  and a half where you can emulate a loft  on top is  fundamental in small designs by pushing  that ceiling height up  as high as possible you also get that  added benefit of creating the windows  in the ceiling so now you can implement  some skylights into your design  to genuinely let that natural light flow  through  and fill it with light the more light a  small home has  the bigger it looks the less light it  has the smaller and confined it looks  so you definitely want to try get as  much light in through the walls  through the ceiling and by having as  much space  for that light to come into now if you  do have an abundance of natural light  coming in  from every direction you don't want to  have a dark moody small home  it just can't be done effectively i'm  sure people have tried it  but i guarantee you if you have a light  vibrant  small home it's always going to look  bigger it might appear like an optical  illusion but light reflects better  off light colors it isn't absorbed into  the darkness  so by having light bright walls either  whites light grays light beiges any  timeless color you can think of  it is always going to appear bigger so  you never want to go with the dark  colors no matter how trendy they are  no matter how much you love them you  want to stick to the light bright colors  and finally you do not want to feel like  you're tucked away in a small home  you always want to have some sort of  connection to nature  greenery is pivotal in any small home  design  a pot plant here and a pot plant there  is all well and good but you really want  to try create that  indoor outdoor connection so think about  the window seals think about the outside  of the building how it can be created  and pushed back inside the building if  there's an ability to create a planter  in front of one of your main windows  then you can easily replicate nature  coming out of the ground  and into the home it is a well  researched statement that greenery and  nature  genuinely makes people happier singapore  for example is  trying to improve their forestry in  cities by  doubling it as much as they can every  single year  to ensure people have a genuine sense of  happiness  so living in a small home living in  something that you might feel  claustrophobic in  you do want to emulate as much nature as  possible to really trigger  those psychological emotions you want to  stay happy in your home  you want to stay healthy in your home  and you want to genuinely love it as  much as possible  anyway that's all for me today guys i  hope this advice helped you on your  small home design journey or on your  renovation journey  small homes are a challenge take as much  time as you need  and make sure you master plan everything  anyway that's all for me today guys  thanks so much for watching i hope you  enjoyed the video  if you did make sure you smash that  subscribe button down below and because  this video forms one of my 28 videos in  28 days  it would usually be i'll see you next  monday but in this case  i'll see you tomorrow

